\question{8}{8} On a certain day, the temperature at 2 p.m.\ is $25.8^\circ$C. The temperature changes after each hour from 2 p.m.\ to 5 p.m.\ are recorded as follows. (A decrease in temperature is represented by a negative number.)

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Time} & \textbf{Temperature change ($^\circ$C)} \\
\hline
2 p.m.\ to 3 p.m. & $-1.7$ \\
3 p.m.\ to 4 p.m. & $+0.9$ \\
4 p.m.\ to 5 p.m. & $-1.35$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{subparts}
  \item Find the temperatures at
  \begin{subpartsii}[1]
    \item 3 p.m.,
    \item 4 p.m.,
    \item 5 p.m.
  \end{subpartsii}

  \item If the temperature at 8 p.m.\ is $21.75^\circ$C and the temperature changes from 5 p.m.\ to 6 p.m., from 6 p.m.\ to 7 p.m.\ and from 7 p.m.\ to 8 p.m.\ form an arithmetic sequence whose common difference is $-0.25^\circ$C, find the three hourly changes and decide whether the temperature was ever above $25.8^\circ$C. Explain your answer.
\end{subparts}

\question{9}{8} It is given that $T_n=n^2$ is the general term of a sequence.

\begin{subparts}
  \item Determine what kind of sequence $T_1,T_2,T_3,\ldots$ is, and write down its first four terms.

  \item The sum of the first $n$ terms of the sequence is given by the following formula.
  \[
  S_n = \sum_{k=1}^{n} T_k = \dfrac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}
  \]
  \begin{subpartsii}[1]
    \item Find the values of $S_{15}$, $S_{24}$ and $S_{25}$.

    \item Hence, find the values of $2^2+4^2+6^2+\cdots+20^2$ and $1^3+2^3+3^3+\cdots+24^3$.
  \end{subpartsii}
\end{subparts}
